5. API Key Configuration
FitLog's AI features are powered by Google Gemini. A free API key (with a generous daily quota) is available from Google AI Studio.
Obtaining a Key
5.	Navigate to https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
6.	Sign in with a Google account.
7.	Click Create API Key.
8.	Copy the generated key.
Configuring FitLog
9.	In FitLog, click the ⚙️ (Settings) icon in the top navigation bar.
10.	Locate the Gemini API Key field.
11.	Paste the key into the password-type input.
12.	Press 💾 Save API Key. The key is validated and a status indicator confirms it is active.
Storage & Security
The key is stored exclusively in the browser's localStorage under a dedicated key. It is transmitted only to Google's Gemini API endpoint when the user explicitly invokes an AI feature. It is never sent to any server operated by FitLog or its author.
⚠️  Security Note
Because localStorage is accessible to JavaScript running on the same origin, treat your API key as a secret. Do not share your browser profile or exported localStorage data with others.
If you suspect the key has been exposed, regenerate it immediately at Google AI Studio and update FitLog's Settings.


6. Core Modules
FitLog is divided into five primary feature modules, accessible via the bottom navigation bar.
6.1  Dashboard  📊
The Dashboard is the application's home screen. It provides a high-level summary of the user's activity for the current day.
Metrics Displayed
•	Daily Calories — Total kilocalories consumed and burned.
•	Protein — Grams of protein logged for the day.
•	Workouts — Number of workout sessions completed.
•	Streak — Current consecutive active-day streak (see Section 7).
•	Steps — Step count (sourced from Google Fit or Mi Band if connected, otherwise manual).
•	Hydration — Water intake in millilitres against a configurable daily target.
•	Weight — Today's logged body weight (kg), with quick-entry modal.
Quick-Log Controls
The Dashboard provides one-tap access to the Water modal (250 ml / 500 ml / 750 ml / 1000 ml presets, or a custom value) and the Weight modal (−0.5 / Same / +0.5 kg quick buttons, or a manual input).
6.2  Nutrition Logging  🍽️
The Nutrition module provides four distinct pathways for logging food intake, catering to different use-case scenarios.
A. AI Chat Logging
The most natural input method. The user types or speaks a description of a meal (e.g. "I had two boiled eggs, a slice of toast with butter, and a glass of orange juice"). FitLog sends the description to the Gemini API, which returns structured JSON containing estimated calories, protein, carbohydrates, and fat. The result is parsed and added to the day's nutrition log.
B. Barcode Scanning
Powered by html5-qrcode (loaded from CDN) and the Open Food Facts API. The user opens the scanner via Settings > Open Scanner or the scan button in the Nutrition section. The camera activates and continuously scans for a barcode. On detection, FitLog queries the Open Food Facts REST API with the barcode value and retrieves the product's nutritional data per 100 g. The user confirms the serving size and the item is logged.
C. Photo-Based Meal Analysis
The user captures or uploads a photo of their meal. FitLog sends the image (base64-encoded) to the Gemini Vision API with a structured prompt requesting nutrition estimation. The AI returns an approximate calorie and macronutrient breakdown which is presented to the user for confirmation before logging.
D. Favorites & Quick Logging
Frequently logged items can be saved as Favorites for one-tap re-entry. Favourites are stored in localStorage and rendered in the Nutrition log view as quick-action cards.
Food Search & Custom Foods
The Food Search modal queries Open Food Facts by name. Users can also create Custom Foods — a named entry with manually entered calories, protein, carbohydrates, fat, and serving size. Custom foods appear in a dedicated list within the Food Search modal.
6.3  Workout Studio  🏋️
The Workout Studio is the most complex module in FitLog. It covers the full lifecycle of a workout plan: AI generation, preview, editing, execution, and manual logging.
AI-Generated Multi-Week Plans
The user specifies goals, fitness level, available equipment, and workout days per week. FitLog constructs a detailed prompt and submits it to the Gemini API. The AI returns a structured JSON plan defining exercises, sets, reps, and rest durations for each day across multiple weeks.
Plan Preview Modal
Before accepting an AI-generated plan, the user sees a full preview: each week, each day, and each exercise with its prescribed sets and reps. The user can Accept the plan or Discard it and request a new generation.
Edit Mode
Once a plan is accepted and active, the user can enter Edit Mode on any day. In this mode:
•	Exercise names can be changed via the Exercise Picker.
•	Sets and reps can be edited inline.
•	Individual days can be regenerated by AI (AI Regenerate).
•	Days can be marked as rest days.
Exercise Picker
A searchable modal listing exercises categorised by muscle group. Used to swap a planned exercise for an alternative. Typing in the search field filters the list in real time.
Active Workout Mode
Tapping Start on a scheduled day launches the full-screen Active Workout Mode. This mode presents each exercise as a slide. For each exercise:
•	The exercise name, prescribed sets × reps, and a GIF demonstration are shown.
•	A rest timer (ring animation with countdown) triggers automatically between sets.
•	The user can tap Skip to bypass the rest timer or +15s to extend it.
•	Navigation arrows (Prev / Next) and a Done button are available.
•	A progress bar tracks completion across all exercises.
•	An elapsed-time timer runs in the header.
On completion, a Congratulations overlay celebrates the finished session, increments the streak, and records the workout.
Plan Start / Stop / Pause
Active plans can be paused (preserving the current week and day position) or stopped (clearing the plan). A stopped plan can be regenerated fresh.
Quick Workout Generator
For users who prefer spontaneous sessions, the Quick Workout Generator produces a single-day workout without a multi-week plan context.
Manual Workout Logging
Users can log any workout manually by selecting a date, entering exercises, sets, reps, and optional notes. These entries are stored alongside AI-plan workout sessions.
Exercise History Modal
For any exercise, tapping its name opens a history modal showing all previous logged instances: date, sets, reps, and progression over time.
6.4  Body Metrics  📐
US Navy Body Fat Calculator
Implements the US Navy circumference method for estimating body fat percentage. For males: neck and waist measurements (cm). For females: neck, waist, and hip measurements (cm) plus height (cm). The formula produces an estimated body fat percentage displayed alongside an interpretation (e.g. Fitness, Acceptable, Obese).
AI Photo Body Fat Estimation
The user uploads or captures a full-body photo. FitLog sends the image to the Gemini Vision API with a body-fat-estimation prompt. The AI returns an estimated percentage range. This feature is clearly labelled as an approximation and not a clinical measurement.
TDEE & Macro Calculator
Accepts age, sex, height, weight, and activity level, then calculates:
•	BMR (Basal Metabolic Rate) via the Mifflin-St Jeor equation.
•	TDEE (Total Daily Energy Expenditure) by applying an activity multiplier.
•	Recommended macro split (protein, carbohydrates, fat) in grams based on configurable goals (maintenance, cut, bulk).
6.5  Progress Charts  📈
The Progress section renders interactive charts for long-term tracking. Charts are implemented in pure Canvas / SVG without an external charting library.
•	Weight Over Time — Plots daily weigh-ins as a line chart. Highlights trend direction.
•	Weekly Calorie Intake vs Burned — A grouped bar chart comparing calories consumed against an estimated calories-burned value derived from workouts and TDEE, grouped by calendar week.

7. Streak System  🔥
The Streak System incentivises consistent training by tracking consecutive active days.
How Streaks Work
A streak increments when the user completes a workout session (via Active Workout Mode or by manually logging a workout) on a given calendar day. Consecutive days with logged workouts build the streak. Missing a day that was scheduled as a training day breaks the streak — unless a Streak Freeze is consumed.
Streak Freezes
Streak freezes are automatically calculated based on the user's selected plan frequency:
Freeze Count = 7 − (days/week selected in plan)
For example, a user on a 5-day plan receives 2 freezes per week. If a non-rest day is missed and a freeze is available, the freeze is automatically consumed to maintain the streak. The freeze count resets each week.
Dashboard Display
The current streak count is shown prominently on the Dashboard. A fire emoji (🔥) is displayed alongside the count for visual reinforcement. The streak resets to zero if a training day is missed without an available freeze.

8. Integrations
8.1  Google Gemini AI
All AI features share a common API call pattern: the user's Gemini API key (stored in localStorage) is attached to an HTTP POST to the Gemini REST endpoint. The request body includes the model name and a content array containing the user prompt and, where applicable, base64-encoded image data.
Models Used
•	gemini-pro — text-only tasks: meal description parsing, workout plan generation.
•	gemini-pro-vision — multimodal tasks: photo meal analysis, photo body-fat estimation.
Error Handling
If the API returns an error (invalid key, quota exceeded, network failure), FitLog displays an inline error message prompting the user to check their key or try again. No partial data is committed to the log on failure.
8.2  Open Food Facts API
Open Food Facts is a free, open food products database with over 3 million items. FitLog uses two endpoints:
•	Barcode lookup: GET /api/v2/product/{barcode}.json — fetches product by EAN/UPC barcode.
•	Name search: GET /cgi/search.pl?search_terms={query} — returns a list of matching products.
No API key is required for Open Food Facts. Data returned includes product name, brand, energy (kcal per 100 g), protein, carbohydrates, fat, and optional fields like fibre and sodium.
8.3  Google Fit Integration
Google Fit integration uses OAuth 2.0 (browser-side Google Sign-In). Once connected:
•	Steps for the current day are fetched and displayed on the Dashboard.
•	Weight entries logged in FitLog can be written back to Google Fit.
•	Calorie burn data from Google Fit can supplement FitLog's burned-calories estimate.
Connect / Disconnect Flow
The Settings modal contains a dedicated Google Fit section with a Sign in with Google button, a sync summary grid (showing last-sync details), a Sync Now button, and a Disconnect button. The status dot (green = connected, grey = not connected) provides at-a-glance status.
8.4  Mi Band / BLE Fitness Band
FitLog uses the Web Bluetooth API to connect to Xiaomi Mi Band 2/3/4/5 or any BLE device exposing the Step Counter characteristic (UUID 0x2A5B).
•	Supported browsers: Chrome and Edge on Android, Chrome on desktop (with experimental Bluetooth flags enabled on some platforms).
•	The Connect Band button in Settings triggers the browser's Bluetooth device picker.
•	Once connected, the step count is polled and synced to the Dashboard steps metric.
•	The Disconnect button cleanly terminates the BLE session.
The Mi Band section in Settings shows a status dot, a status label, and the timestamp of the last successful sync.

9. Cloud Sync & Data Export
Sync Code Mechanism
FitLog does not have a proprietary backend. Instead, it uses a sync-code mechanism: the user's localStorage data is serialised to JSON and stored against a short alphanumeric sync code on a lightweight cloud service. The user can share the code across devices to restore their data.
Backup Flow
13.	Open Settings > ☁️ Open Cloud Sync.
14.	Generate a new sync code (or use the existing one shown).
15.	Press ☁️ Backup Now. The data is uploaded and associated with the code.
16.	Copy the code (📋 Copy button) and keep it safe.
Restore Flow
17.	On a new device, open Cloud Sync.
18.	Enter the sync code in the Restore from Code field.
19.	Press Restore. The data is downloaded and written to localStorage.
JSON Export
The Export JSON button downloads a complete snapshot of all FitLog localStorage data as a .json file. This file can be used as a manual backup or inspected for debugging purposes.
Cloud Status
Two status indicators (cloud-status-dot and cloud-status-text) appear both inside the Cloud Sync modal and in the main Settings modal for quick access.

